# Assist on the tablet

Wake word detection runs on the tablet. Speech-to-text, the conversation, and the spoken reply stay on the Assist pipeline the screen already uses. Room audio leaves the tablet only after the wake word, for that command.

## Why detection is on the device

Streaming the microphone into Home Assistant's wake-word stage would upload audio all day and hold a pipeline run open. The bundled models are microWakeWord: a micro_speech frontend, then a streaming model and a sliding window. A TensorFlow interpreter on the raw `.tflite` skips that frontend and misses the phrase. The player wraps the same JNI engine the Home Assistant companion uses (`player/src/main/cpp/microwakeword`). The JNI class is `tech.shaffersoftworks.remotescreens.player.microwakeword.MicroWakeWord`. Each model ships its own `probability_cutoff`, `sliding_window_size`, and `feature_step_size`; those construct the detector. Bundled phrases are okay_nabu (default), hey_jarvis, hey_mycroft, and alexa. A custom openWakeWord-only phrase is not heard.

Native libraries are `arm64-v8a` and `armeabi-v7a` only.

## Why the pipeline starts at speech-to-text

After a hit the engine resets and ignores the wake word for two seconds (`COOLDOWN_CHUNKS`, 100 chunks of the 20 ms capture, the same window the Home Assistant Android app uses). The microphone stays open. After the wake sound finishes, command audio from that same capture goes to the pipeline until Home Assistant is thinking or speaking, then the capture keeps feeding only the wake word. The tablet plays the Home Assistant Voice Preview wake sound through `AudioTrack` from `wake_word_triggered_cue.pcm` (CC BY 4.0, Clayton Charles Tapp). The FLAC next to it is the licensed source. A failure is logged as `AssistChime` and the turn still starts. Do not replace it with a generated tone. A new engine, opened when listening starts or the configuration changes, is warmed with four seconds of quiet noise (`MicroWakeWord.warmUp`, 400 chunks, the companion's warm-up) before the microphone opens. A cold frontend misses the next phrase. Do not close the engine after each turn, and do not open a second `AudioRecord` for the reply. The add-on then opens `assist_pipeline/run` with `start_stage: stt` and `end_stage: tts`. Audio from before the hit is not sent. Binary PCM goes to `stt_binary_handler_id` from the `run-start` event. The handler id is one byte in front of each PCM frame. An empty frame ends the utterance. The pipeline's own wake-word stage is not used. A follow-up (`continue_conversation`) is another speech-to-text run with the same `conversation_id`, after the reply finishes, and then local listening starts again. `run-end`, a timeout, or an error closes `/assist` and returns to the local detector. It does not start a Home Assistant wake-word run.

Home Assistant sends JSON null for an absent object. `JsonElement.jsonObject` throws on that, and Ktor then closes `/assist` before `ended`, so the tablet never gets back to the wake loop. `parsePipelineMessage` uses `jsonObjectOrNull()` (`this as? JsonObject`). `?.jsonObject` does not skip null. An add-on build that still calls `getJsonObject` on those events keeps killing the socket.

The tablet socket is `/assist` on the player port. It opens for one turn. PCM is binary, not a `Protocol` text message. The add-on uses a separate Home Assistant websocket for the run because `HomeAssistantClient` reads text only and owns state.

## Why the microphone control is native

The conversation is drawn by the player, not Remote Compose, so a partial transcript does not rebuild the document. When Assist is on, `topBarTrailing` draws a microphone with the other status icons, first in that row, the same 26 dp as wifi and battery. A tap is the local verb `assist` (`ActionRef("_", "assist")`) and calls `toggleAssistMute()`. The player swaps `microphone` and `microphone-off` through `AssistLayout.MIC_VARIABLE` (`sys.assist_mic`) and `MUTED_VARIABLE` (`sys.assist_muted`) on `RemoteScreenState.localInts`, so mute does not rebuild the document. There is no header-mic component id and no Compose overlay for it. An add-on built before that icon does not send it; do not paint a second microphone over the bar. The editor mockup shows the same icon when `screen.settings.assist`. The Assist page is a hole (`LiveAssist`, component id `0x62_0000`) the same way the energy dashboard is. The wake word does not change pages. On any other page a snackbar above the nav shows what was heard and the reply. It clears about a second after the audio stops, and also when the socket drops. It does not paint over the page.

The spoken reply is a short `audio/mpeg` byte array. ExoPlayer often stays in `BUFFERING` after the `AudioTrack` has stopped and reports a duration much longer than the clip. `PlaybackEnd` finishes one second after playback stops (`PLAYBACK_TAIL`), including that stall. Waiting on `STATE_ENDED` or on the reported duration holds the snackbar up. Do not set a mime type or `setAudioAttributes` on that player; that change was reverted. The wake sound does not use that player. On the office keypad (px30, Android 8.1) ExoPlayer scans codecs and releases the FLAC without creating an `AudioTrack`, while the spoken reply still plays. Holding the player open until the playhead moved did not create a track either. `playCue` writes `player/src/main/res/raw/wake_word_triggered_cue.pcm` (the same 48 kHz 16-bit mono recording, 90910 bytes) to a static `AudioTrack` on `USAGE_MEDIA` and waits the clip length plus `CUE_TAIL_MS`. A failure is logged as `AssistChime` and the turn still starts. The FLAC stays in the repo as the licensed source. Android raw resources drop the file extension, so the PCM file is `wake_word_triggered_cue`, not a second `wake_word_triggered`. This cue was heard on that keypad and on the Samsung SM-T725. A device-owner player installs a higher `versionCode` with no prompt and the process is killed. Released 0.2.68 (versionCode 268) replaced a 0.2.67 debug build that way and the cue went silent again, because 0.2.68 still plays the FLAC through ExoPlayer. A test build has to use a `versionCode` at least as high as the installed release or the keypad puts the release back.

Assist is off until the screen setting `assist` is on. Mute, and an intercom call, stop the detector and release the mic; Assist resumes after the call, and that new engine is warmed before it listens. Saying the wake word during the command or the reply cancels playback and the socket, drops the snackbar, plays the wake sound, and starts another turn on the same capture. The two-second cooldown is what stops the phrase that just hit from starting a second turn. `AcousticEchoCanceler` and `NoiseSuppressor` attach on the one capture when the device has them. There is no echo-cancellation JNI.

Dimmed standby keeps listening. Real screen-off uses `AssistListenService` (`foregroundServiceType="microphone"`) plus the partial wake lock the player already holds. Some panels still drop the microphone when the display is off.

Demo mode shows the sample conversation and does not open a pipeline or the microphone. Sendspin is not part of this path.
